<rml>
<!--
  Issue #29 freeze repro. The inline <script> below deliberately HARD-LOOPS on
  load. Open this scene's RmlContext in the live-preview panel:
    - "Run inline scripts" OFF (default): document renders, editing the RML
      stays smooth, _boom() never runs. This is the fix.
    - "Run inline scripts" ON: the loop runs in the editor process and HANGS it
      (you must kill Godot). This proves the script genuinely freezes the editor
      and that the off-by-default gate is what protects you.
  Not shipped in examples on purpose — it's a destructive test fixture.
-->
<head>
	<title>Freeze Repro</title>
	<style>
		body {
			font-family: "Noto Sans";
			font-size: 18px;
			color: #eeeeee;
			background-color: #2a1d1d;
			width: 100%;
			height: 100%;
			padding: 24px;
		}
		h1 { font-size: 24px; color: #ff8a8a; margin-bottom: 8px; }
		p  { color: #ddc; margin-bottom: 8px; }
		code { color: #ffd479; }
	</style>
	<script>
	var rml_context

	func _boom(_event):
		# Infinite loop — if this runs in the editor, it freezes. The gate keeps
		# it from running unless "Run inline scripts" is ticked.
		while true:
			pass
	</script>
</head>
<body onload="gdscript:_boom">
	<h1>Freeze Repro (issue #29)</h1>
	<p>Edit this text — it should stay responsive while
	<code>Run inline scripts</code> is OFF.</p>
	<p>Tick the checkbox to (intentionally) hang the editor.</p>
</body>
</rml>
